WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.584
Cloud GPUs exchange hardware ownership for flexible access to remote acceleration.

00:00:05.584 --> 00:00:16.083
They are especially useful for temporary demand, experiments, burst capacity, shared team environments, and hardware configurations that would be difficult to purchase.

00:00:16.083 --> 00:00:27.475
Local hardware may remain simpler or less expensive for steady workloads, sensitive local data, and users who need guaranteed access to a known system.

00:00:27.475 --> 00:00:31.273
Select an instance as a complete platform.

00:00:31.273 --> 00:00:44.452
The GPU model and VRAM must fit the workload, while virtual CPUs, RAM, storage, network capacity, region, quota, and actual availability affect whether the system works in practice.

00:00:44.452 --> 00:00:52.270
Calculate full-lifecycle cost, including idle time, disks, snapshots, object storage, data transfer, addresses, and operational labor.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:58.748
Use commitments only for predictable demand and interruption discounts only for restartable work.

00:00:58.748 --> 00:01:01.652
Make the software stack reproducible.

00:01:01.652 --> 00:01:10.364
Pin images and packages, verify acceleration, protect management interfaces, and test inference with representative prompts, contexts, and concurrency.

00:01:10.364 --> 00:01:13.268
Measure latency and throughput separately.

00:01:13.268 --> 00:01:23.320
Treat autoscaling as an operational system with startup delay and spending limits, not as an automatic cure for poor capacity planning.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.564
Finally, protect access and data throughout the lifecycle.

00:01:27.564 --> 00:01:35.382
Apply least-privilege IAM, private networking, authenticated APIs, secrets management, encryption, retention rules, monitoring, and incident procedures.

00:01:35.382 --> 00:01:44.541
Stop or delete compute when it is no longer needed, then account for storage and network artifacts that remain.

00:01:44.541 --> 00:01:57.273
Successful cloud GPU use means renting the right capacity for a defined job, controlling its data and cost while it runs, and leaving no accidental exposure behind.
